/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Modern History (batch y) */
WB.L("spanish-armada", "history", "beginner", "The Spanish Armada, 1588",
  "The most powerful empire on Earth sent a giant fleet to conquer England. Fireships, bad maps and brutal storms decided what happened next.",
  [
    ["A quarrel between monarchs", "The Armada grew from a religious and political feud between Catholic Spain and Protestant England.",
      "Picture two rulers who had once been in-laws. Philip II of Spain had been married to Mary I of England, Elizabeth I's older half-sister. After Mary died, Elizabeth steered England firmly towards Protestantism, while Philip saw himself as the champion of Catholic Europe. The quarrel deepened for decades. English privateers such as Francis Drake raided Spanish treasure ships and colonies, and Elizabeth sent soldiers to help Dutch Protestants rebelling against Spanish rule.\n\nThe breaking point came in 1587, when Elizabeth allowed the execution of Mary, Queen of Scots, the Catholic cousin many hoped would replace her. To Philip, invading England now looked like a matter of faith, security and honour all at once."],
    ["The grand plan", "The Armada was meant to escort an invasion army across the Channel, not conquer England alone.",
      "The Armada that sailed in 1588 numbered around 130 ships, carrying sailors, soldiers, priests, horses and enormous supplies. Its commander, the Duke of Medina Sidonia, was a wealthy nobleman with little experience at sea who had reportedly tried to decline the job. The fleet could not conquer England by itself. Its task was to sail up the English Channel, link up with the Duke of Parma's veteran army in the Spanish Netherlands, and protect the barges carrying those troops to Kent.\n\nThe flaw was plain in hindsight. No deep-water port existed where the big ships and Parma's barges could safely meet, and messages between the two dukes moved slowly. Coordinating them under enemy fire would prove almost impossible."],
    ["Fireships at Calais", "The English could not break the Armada's formation until fireships scattered it off Calais.",
      "In late July 1588 the Armada was sighted off Cornwall and beacons flared along the coast. It moved up the Channel in a disciplined crescent, with strong warships guarding the supply vessels at the centre. The English, led by Lord Howard of Effingham with Drake as a senior captain, had faster ships and longer-range guns. Spanish tactics relied on boarding, so the English kept their distance. The Armada stayed largely intact and anchored off Calais.\n\nOn the night of 7 to 8 August, by the modern calendar, the English sent eight burning ships, packed with tar and pitch, drifting towards the anchored fleet. Many Spanish captains cut their cables and scattered. At Gravelines the English attacked closer, wrecking or capturing several ships and pushing the rest away from Parma."],
    ["The long way home", "Storms, hunger and disease on the voyage around Scotland and Ireland cost Spain more than English guns did.",
      "With the wind from the south and the English behind them, the Spanish could not return down the Channel. Medina Sidonia sailed north around Scotland and down the west coast of Ireland, a huge detour through unfamiliar, stormy waters. The ships were battered and short of water and food, and their charts of those coasts were poor. Autumn gales drove many onto the rocks of Ireland and Scotland. Thousands of men drowned, and many who reached shore were killed or captured.\n\nHistorians think roughly a third or more of the fleet never made it back to Spain, and a far larger share of the men died. Most of those losses came from weather, disease and hunger rather than from battle."],
    ["Legend and reality", "The Armada did not end Spanish power, but it became a founding story of English defiance.",
      "While the danger was still real, Elizabeth travelled to Tilbury on the Thames to review her troops. Accounts record her saying she had the body of a weak and feeble woman, but the heart and stomach of a king. Historians debate her exact words, because the best-known version was written down years later. English propaganda presented the victory as God's favour and called the storms a Protestant wind.\n\nThe reality was messier. The war dragged on until 1604. An English counter-attack in 1589 failed badly, and Philip's later armadas in the 1590s were scattered by storms. Spain stayed dominant in Europe for decades. Yet the failure kept Protestant England independent, encouraged the Dutch revolt and gave English sailors confidence. Its biggest legacy was a story of plucky defiance, retold in 1805, 1940 and beyond."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What finally pushed Philip II to treat an invasion of England as a matter of faith, security and honour?", ["The death of Mary I","Elizabeth allowing the execution of Mary, Queen of Scots","The founding of an English colony in Virginia","Drake's voyage around the world"], 1, "Years of raids and rebellion culminated in the 1587 execution of the Catholic Mary, Queen of Scots."],
    [1, "What was the Armada's main job?", ["To blockade London and starve it into surrender","To capture Ireland as a base for attack","To escort the Duke of Parma's army across the Channel to England","To seize the English treasure fleet at sea"], 2, "The fleet was meant to protect the barges carrying Parma's troops, not to conquer England alone."],
    [2, "What broke up the Armada's formation off Calais?", ["A sudden fog","A mutiny on the flagship","A night attack with fireships","Cannon fire from the French shore"], 2, "Burning ships drifting into the anchored fleet terrified Spanish captains, who cut their cables and scattered."],
    [3, "What caused most of the Armada's losses?", ["Storms, shipwrecks, hunger and disease on the way home","English boarding parties in close combat","Dutch privateers attacking stragglers","A plague outbreak in the Spanish ports"], 0, "The detour around Scotland and Ireland in autumn gales killed far more men than battle did."],
    [4, "What happened after 1588?", ["Spain surrendered and signed peace immediately","England invaded and conquered Spain in 1589","The war dragged on to 1604 and Spain stayed powerful","Spain never built another large fleet"], 2, "The Armada's defeat did not end Spanish power; the myth of a decisive victory is simpler than the reality."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The mightiest fleet in Europe was beaten, but not mainly by English guns. By what?"});

WB.L("great-fire-of-london", "history", "beginner", "The Great Fire of London",
  "In September 1666 a small fire in a bakery grew into a firestorm that swallowed a medieval city. What rose from the ashes still shapes London today.",
  [
    ["A city built to burn", "A small bakery fire in 1666 spread because London's timber houses, narrow lanes and warehouses were perfect fuel.",
      "Sometime after midnight on Sunday 2 September 1666, a fire started in Thomas Farriner's bakery on Pudding Lane, near London Bridge. Farriner insisted he had put out his oven, but the house was soon ablaze. The Lord Mayor, Sir Thomas Bludworth, reportedly decided the blaze was not worth demolishing neighbouring houses for. It was a costly misjudgement.\n\nLondon was still largely medieval. Houses were timber and plaster, with overhanging upper storeys that almost touched across narrow streets. Rubbish, hay and firewood lay everywhere. Firefighting meant leather buckets, axes, hand pumps and hooks for pulling buildings down, with no organised brigade. The summer had been dry and a strong east wind was blowing. When the fire reached warehouses on the Thames full of oil, tallow, coal and brandy, it grew too hot to approach."],
    ["Four days, two diarists", "The fire raged until Wednesday and was stopped by firebreaks, and diaries preserve vivid human details.",
      "For about four days the fire swept west and north. People fled with what they could carry, piling belongings into carts and boats. St Paul's Cathedral, covered in wooden scaffolding for repairs, caught fire, and its lead roof reportedly ran down the streets. King Charles II and his brother James, Duke of York, helped create firebreaks by pulling down or blowing up houses. When the wind eased on Tuesday night, the firebreaks held, and the fire was under control by Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nWe know much of this from Samuel Pepys and John Evelyn. Pepys watched from a boat, noting pigeons that hovered around their homes until their wings caught fire. He also buried his wine and parmesan cheese in his garden. Evelyn described the ground so hot it was hard to walk on."],
    ["Counting the cost", "The fire destroyed most of the old city, and fear led to scapegoating.",
      "About 13,000 houses were destroyed, along with 87 parish churches, St Paul's Cathedral, the Guildhall and many other public buildings. Tens of thousands of people were homeless, camping in the fields north of the city. Only a handful of deaths were officially recorded, but historians think the true number was higher, since the poor, or anyone consumed by intense heat, may never have been counted.\n\nPeople wanted someone to blame. Foreigners and Catholics were attacked in the streets. A French watchmaker named Robert Hubert confessed to starting the fire and was hanged, even though evidence later suggested he had not been in London when it began. Fear ran high: London had lost perhaps a fifth of its people to the Great Plague of 1665. An inquiry eventually concluded the fire was an accident."],
    ["Rebuilding London", "London was rebuilt on its old street pattern, but with brick and stone and new safety rules.",
      "Within days, Christopher Wren and John Evelyn presented plans for a new London with wide boulevards and open piazzas. None was adopted. Property owners wanted their land back quickly, and sorting out who owned what was hard enough already. The city was rebuilt largely along its old street pattern, with important changes. New laws required buildings of brick or stone, set limits on heights and widened some streets.\n\nWren designed the new St Paul's Cathedral with its famous dome, which took decades to complete, and dozens of new city churches. A special Fire Court settled disputes between landlords and tenants so rebuilding could get moving."],
    ["The long shadow", "The fire left a monument, helped launch fire insurance and shaped modern fire safety.",
      "Near Pudding Lane stands the Monument, a stone column designed by Wren and Robert Hooke. It is 202 feet, roughly 61 metres, high, and stands about 202 feet from where the bakery was. The fire also helped launch modern property insurance: in the following years businesses began offering cover against fire, and some ran their own teams of firefighters to protect insured buildings.\n\nA popular story says the fire ended the plague by burning the rats, but historians doubt it. The plague was already declining and also faded in areas the fire never touched. What is certain is that the disaster changed how cities thought about fire safety, building materials and planning, lessons that later shaped building codes around the world, including in Australia."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did flames spread so easily between London's houses?", ["Timber houses packed along narrow lanes","Most houses had thatched roofs and stone walls","The streets were paved with wooden blocks and tar","Gas lamps exploded along the main streets"], 0, "Timber buildings, close-packed lanes and a strong wind let the fire leap from one side of a street to the other."],
    [1, "How was the fire finally brought under control?", ["Heavy rain fell on the city for two days","Houses were pulled down or blown up as firebreaks, and the wind eased","The Thames was diverted into the burning streets","A trained fire brigade pumped water from the river"], 1, "Demolishing buildings created gaps the flames could not cross once the wind dropped."],
    [2, "Why was Robert Hubert's confession doubtful?", ["Evidence suggested he was not in London when it began","He later recanted his confession before a jury","He was a trained baker with no clear motive","The fire had actually begun on a different street"], 0, "Fear led to scapegoating, and an inquiry later concluded the fire was an accident."],
    [3, "What did new building rules require after the fire?", ["Wooden houses with fire buckets","Thatched roofs soaked in water","Houses no taller than one storey","Buildings made of brick or stone"], 3, "London was rebuilt largely on its old street plan, but with brick and stone and limits on heights."],
    [4, "Why do historians doubt that the fire ended the plague?", ["It was already fading, even where the fire never reached","The fire actually spread the plague even further","The plague had already ended several years before 1666","Records show the fire killed every rat in London"], 0, "A tidy story is not good evidence; the plague faded in places the fire never touched."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A single oven started it. Why was a medieval city so ready to burn in 1666?"});

WB.L("east-india-company", "history", "intermediate", "The East India Company: a business that ruled",
  "It began as a group of London merchants chasing pepper. Within two centuries it had its own army and ruled millions of people across India.",
  [
    ["Merchants and a charter", "The East India Company began in 1600 as a monopoly trading venture and turned to Indian textiles after losing the spice islands.",
      "On the last day of 1600, Elizabeth I granted a charter to a group of London merchants, giving them the sole English right to trade with Asia. Their target was spices, especially pepper. The Company was an early joint stock venture: investors pooled money to fund risky voyages and shared the returns. The richer, more aggressive Dutch East India Company, founded in 1602, soon pushed the English out of the most lucrative spice islands in what is now Indonesia.\n\nSo the English turned to India, where they found something more valuable than pepper: cotton textiles. Indian calicoes and chintzes became a fashion craze at home. Its fortified trading posts, called factories, grew into Madras (now Chennai), Bombay (Mumbai) and Calcutta (Kolkata). At first it depended on the favour of the Mughal Empire."],
    ["Betrayal at Plassey", "In 1757 Robert Clive won Bengal for the Company largely through intrigue, because key commanders had already switched sides.",
      "As Mughal power weakened in the 1700s, the Company began to use soldiers and money to play politics. Bengal was among India's richest provinces. In 1756 its young ruler, the Nawab Siraj ud-Daulah, captured Calcutta after the Company strengthened its fortifications without permission. Robert Clive, an ambitious and ruthless Company officer, sailed north to retake it.\n\nHe found allies among Bengal's elite who wanted the Nawab gone, including his army commander Mir Jafar. At the Battle of Plassey in 1757, Clive's much smaller force faced the Nawab's army, but much of it never fought because its commanders had already agreed to switch sides. Siraj ud-Daulah was later killed and Mir Jafar installed as a puppet ruler. Huge sums flowed to the Company and to Clive personally."],
    ["Trader to tax collector", "In 1765 the right to collect Bengal's taxes turned the Company from a trader into a government.",
      "The real turning point came in 1765. The weakened Mughal emperor granted the Company the diwani of Bengal, Bihar and Orissa: the right to collect land revenue across a vast, populous region. Instead of bringing silver from Britain to buy Indian goods, the Company could now use Indian taxes to pay for them, and for its growing army.\n\nPoorly supervised officials often grew rich through private trade and bribes, earning the nickname 'nabobs' back in Britain. In 1770 a devastating famine struck Bengal. Millions died, possibly as much as a third of the population in the worst areas, and critics accused the Company of making things worse by keeping up harsh tax collection while people starved."],
    ["Too big to fail", "Britain's Parliament stepped in as the Company's power and debts became a national problem.",
      "By the early 1770s the Company was both mighty and in financial trouble, and Parliament grew alarmed that a private business was running an empire. The Regulating Act of 1773 began bringing it under government oversight, and later laws created a government board to supervise its political decisions. The 1773 Tea Act, meant to help the Company sell surplus tea in America, led to the Boston Tea Party. Warren Hastings, the first Governor General of Bengal, was impeached in a seven-year trial and acquitted, but it showed British unease.\n\nMeanwhile the Company fought wars against Mysore and the Marathas. Most of its soldiers were Indian sepoys led by British officers, and its armies reached hundreds of thousands, larger than Britain's own. It also grew opium for smuggling into China, to pay for Chinese tea."],
    ["Rebellion and the end", "After the 1857 uprising the British Crown took direct control, and the Company was dissolved.",
      "In 1857 a mutiny by sepoys at Meerut spread into a widespread rebellion across northern India, fuelled by grievances from annexations and taxes to fears of religious interference. The fighting was brutal on both sides, and the British response was savage. Afterwards, Parliament decided the Company could no longer be trusted with India. In 1858 the Crown took direct control, beginning the period known as the Raj, and the Company was formally dissolved in 1874.\n\nBy the 1850s it ruled much of the subcontinent directly or through treaties with princely states, with its trading monopolies stripped away. Its legacy is debated. It left cities, railways planned in its final years and words such as 'loot', borrowed from Hindi. It also warns of a profit-seeking corporation holding unchecked political and military power."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did the East India Company shift its focus to India?", ["Dutch rivals shut it out of the spice trade","Spices had stopped selling in Europe by 1610","The Mughal emperor ordered it to relocate","The charter forbade any trade in Indonesia"], 0, "After losing the spice trade to the Dutch, the Company found cotton textiles were more valuable than pepper."],
    [1, "How did Robert Clive win at Plassey in 1757?", ["With a much larger and better-armed army than his rival","Using new rifles that the Nawab's army did not possess","Key commanders in the Nawab's army had changed sides","By a long naval bombardment of the fort alone"], 2, "Plassey was won more through intrigue than fighting, since much of the Nawab's army never fought."],
    [2, "What was the diwani the Company gained in 1765?", ["The right to collect taxes across Bengal","A monopoly on all Indian cotton exports","Ownership of the port of Bombay","A permanent seat at the Mughal court"], 0, "Collecting taxes turned a trading company into a government with its own revenue."],
    [3, "Which American event was linked to the Company's troubles?", ["The Salem witch trials","The Louisiana Purchase","The Boston Tea Party","The California gold rush"], 2, "The 1773 Tea Act tried to help the struggling Company sell surplus tea in the colonies and led to the Boston Tea Party."],
    [4, "What happened to India after the 1857 rebellion?", ["It became independent","France took control","The Mughal Empire was restored","The British Crown took direct control"], 3, "Parliament decided the Company could not be trusted, and the Crown began the Raj in 1858."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A pepper-trading firm ended up with its own army. How does a business come to rule?"});

WB.L("meiji-japan", "history", "intermediate", "Meiji Japan: the great makeover",
  "In about forty years, Japan went from a closed realm of samurai and shoguns to an industrial power that defeated a European empire. How did it happen so fast?",
  [
    ["A closed country opens", "After over two centuries of seclusion, American warships in 1853 forced Japan to open up and exposed the shogunate's weakness.",
      "In the early 1600s the Tokugawa family unified Japan and ruled as shoguns, military leaders who held real power while the emperor lived in ceremonial seclusion at Kyoto. Fearing missionaries and European influence, they restricted foreign contact. Japanese were generally forbidden to travel abroad, and European trade was limited to the Dutch on Dejima, a small island in Nagasaki harbour. Japan was not frozen: Edo (now Tokyo) grew huge, literacy was high and the arts flourished. But its rulers did not grasp how fast Western industry was changing.\n\nIn July 1853 four American ships under Commodore Perry steamed into Edo Bay, two of them smoking steamers the Japanese called black ships. Perry demanded open ports. The shogunate signed the Treaty of Kanagawa in 1854, then 'unequal treaties' fixing low tariffs and letting foreigners be tried in their own courts."],
    ["Restoration and the samurai", "In 1868 rebel samurai toppled the shogunate, then dismantled the old class system, including the samurai's own privileges.",
      "Many Japanese were humiliated. Ambitious samurai from Satsuma and Choshu plotted against the Tokugawa under the slogan 'revere the emperor, expel the barbarians'. Clashes with Western warships taught them expelling foreigners was impossible, so Japan would have to learn from the West. The last shogun gave up power, and after a short civil war the emperor was declared restored. He was a teenager named Mutsuhito, and his reign took the name Meiji, meaning 'enlightened rule'. Edo was renamed Tokyo, 'eastern capital'.\n\nThe new leaders moved fast. In 1871 they replaced feudal domains with prefectures and ended samurai stipends. A conscript army replaced the samurai monopoly on fighting, and public sword-wearing was largely banned in 1876. In 1877 the Satsuma Rebellion, led by Saigo Takamori, was crushed by the conscript army."],
    ["Learning from the world", "Japan studied Western nations closely and borrowed selectively from the best of each.",
      "In 1871 the Iwakura Mission, a large government delegation, set off to tour the United States and Europe for well over a year. It visited factories, schools, parliaments, shipyards and prisons, taking careful notes. Japan then borrowed selectively: a navy modelled on Britain's, an army influenced first by France and later by Germany, legal codes drawing on French and German law, and a school system with ideas from several countries.\n\nThe government hired thousands of foreign experts, then gradually replaced them with Japanese graduates. One slogan captured the goal: 'rich country, strong army'. Another described Japanese spirit with Western techniques. Even the calendar changed, as Japan adopted the Western solar calendar from 1873."],
    ["Railways, silk and a constitution", "Industry, schooling and a written constitution turned Japan into a modern state.",
      "Japan's first railway, between Tokyo and Yokohama, opened in 1872. The government built model factories, including a large silk mill at Tomioka, then sold many enterprises cheaply to private businesses. This helped create powerful family conglomerates called zaibatsu, such as Mitsubishi and Mitsui. Silk exports earned the foreign currency needed for machinery and warships. Primary schooling became compulsory.\n\nIn 1889 the emperor granted a written constitution, influenced by Prussia's, which created an elected parliament called the Diet. Voting was limited to a small minority of wealthy men, and the emperor was declared sacred and inviolable. Even so, Japan became the first East Asian nation with a Western-style constitution."],
    ["Power and a warning", "Victories over China and Russia made Japan a great power, but the same drive set it on the road to empire.",
      "Japan's transformation was tested in war. It defeated China in 1894 to 1895 and took Taiwan as a colony. In 1904 to 1905 it shocked the world by defeating Russia on land and at sea, the first time in modern history that an Asian nation beat a European great power in a major war. It also ended foreign extraterritoriality, agreed in 1894, and regained full control of its tariffs in 1911.\n\nWhen the Meiji emperor died in 1912, Japan was an industrial and colonial power that had annexed Korea in 1910. The era is often held up as a model of rapid modernisation. But the same drive for national strength, and the army's growing political power, helped set Japan on a path towards militarism, expansion in Asia and, eventually, the Pacific War."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Perry's 1853 visit expose the shogunate's weakness?", ["It showed the shogun could not resist foreign warships","Perry's fleet captured Edo after a brief battle","The emperor secretly sided with the Americans","Japan lost its trade with China and Korea"], 0, "The shogun seemed unable to protect the country, and his critics rallied around the emperor."],
    [1, "What happened to the samurai class under the Meiji government?", ["It became the core of the new conscript army","It was dismantled, and its stipends and privileges ended","It was given control of the new prefectures","It was strengthened and expanded by the emperor"], 1, "The new leaders, mostly samurai themselves, abolished the old class system and crushed the Satsuma Rebellion."],
    [2, "How did Japan approach learning from other countries?", ["It copied Britain in every single field","It borrowed selectively from the best of several nations","It banned foreign experts from the country","It rejected Western ideas but bought Western weapons"], 1, "Japan modelled its navy on Britain, its army on France and Germany, and its law on French and German systems."],
    [3, "What did the 1889 constitution create?", ["Full voting rights for all adults","A republic","A military government","An elected parliament called the Diet"], 3, "It created a parliament, though only a small minority of wealthy men could vote."],
    [4, "Why was Japan's victory over Russia in 1904 to 1905 so significant?", ["It ended the samurai class and its privileges for good","It was the first modern defeat of a European power by an Asian one","It gave Japan full control of China and Korea","It began the Meiji Restoration of 1868 itself"], 1, "The victory confirmed Japan as a great power, though the same drive pushed it towards militarism."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"In forty years Japan went from samurai to battleships. What made it so fast?"});

WB.L("opium-wars", "history", "advanced", "The Opium Wars and China's century of humiliation",
  "Britain went to war so its merchants could keep selling a drug China had banned. The consequences still shape China's view of the West.",
  [
    ["Tea, silver and opium", "Britain paid for Chinese tea in silver, then used Bengal-grown opium to win that silver back.",
      "By the late 1700s the British drank enormous amounts of tea, almost all of it from China. China wanted little that Britain made, and the Qing government confined Western merchants to the port of Canton, now Guangzhou. So Britain paid in silver, and silver drained towards China.\n\nThe East India Company found a fix in Bengal, which it controlled. It grew opium there and sold it at auction in Calcutta to private traders, who smuggled it ashore in China. That kept the Company's own hands technically clean, and silver began flowing the other way. By the 1830s tens of thousands of chests arrived each year, and addiction had spread from the wealthy to soldiers, officials and labourers."],
    ["Commissioner Lin's stand", "In 1839 Lin Zexu forced foreign merchants to surrender more than 20,000 chests of opium and destroyed them.",
      "China had banned opium, but smugglers worked with corrupt officials. Officials feared the harm to people and the army, and the silver drain, which made taxes harder for farmers to pay. After a fierce debate over legalising or crushing the trade, the Daoguang Emperor chose to crush it.\n\nIn 1839 he sent an incorruptible official, Lin Zexu, to Canton. Lin blockaded the foreign merchants until they surrendered more than 20,000 chests, after British superintendent Charles Elliot promised them compensation. Lin had the opium mixed with lime and salt and flushed out to sea. He also wrote to Queen Victoria, asking how Britain could allow a poison it banned at home to be sold to China. Historians doubt she ever read it."],
    ["The First Opium War", "Britain's modern warships overwhelmed the Qing in 1839 to 1842, and the Treaty of Nanking began China's 'unequal treaties'.",
      "British merchants lobbied for war. Some MPs, including a young William Gladstone, called it unjust and shameful, but the government sent a naval force anyway. Warships such as the iron steamship Nemesis, which could navigate shallow rivers, destroyed Chinese junks and forts, and British forces threatened Nanjing. Misleading reports meant the emperor did not grasp how badly it was going.\n\nThe Treaty of Nanking (1842) was the first of the 'unequal treaties'. China paid an indemnity, handed over Hong Kong Island and opened five ports, including Shanghai. British subjects gained extraterritoriality, meaning trial under British law. Strikingly, the treaty never mentioned opium, and the trade grew."],
    ["The Second Opium War", "A second war, fought by Britain and France, ended in 1860 with the burning of the Old Summer Palace.",
      "Western powers wanted more access, including diplomats in Beijing and trade inland. In 1856 Chinese officials boarded the Arrow, a ship registered in Hong Kong, and Britain used the incident to go to war again, this time with France.\n\nQing officials later detained and mistreated a British and French negotiating party, and some died. In response, the British envoy Lord Elgin ordered the Old Summer Palace outside Beijing destroyed. Troops looted its treasures, many of which remain in Western collections, and burned it. The treaties that followed opened more ports, allowed foreign envoys in Beijing, ceded Kowloon to Britain and effectively legalised the opium trade."],
    ["A century of humiliation", "China calls 1839 to 1949 its century of humiliation, and that memory still shapes how its leaders speak about sovereignty.",
      "The wars weakened the Qing dynasty at a terrible time. The Taiping Rebellion, which began in 1850, killed perhaps 20 million people or more, and further defeats and concessions followed for decades. In 1898 Britain leased the New Territories next to Kowloon for 99 years, which set the stage for Hong Kong's return to Chinese rule in 1997.\n\nIn China, the period from 1839 to the founding of the People's Republic in 1949 is often called the century of humiliation. It features in school textbooks and national messaging. Many in the West know little about the Opium Wars, yet they help explain why China's leaders talk so often about sovereignty, foreign interference and national rejuvenation."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did the East India Company reverse the flow of silver to China?", ["By taxing Chinese tea exports at Canton","By selling British manufactured goods to Chinese buyers","By growing opium in Bengal and selling it to traders who smuggled it in","By banning silver payments for tea"], 2, "Opium grown in Bengal and smuggled into China pulled silver back to Britain's side of the trade."],
    [1, "What worried Qing officials most about the opium trade?", ["Damage to health and the army, and a drain of silver","Opium competing with Chinese tea in foreign markets","Foreign merchants refusing to pay any customs duties","A shortage of opium poppies in the fields of Bengal"], 0, "The trade harmed health and the army, and the loss of silver put pressure on ordinary taxpayers."],
    [2, "What was striking about the Treaty of Nanking, given what started the war?", ["It banned the opium trade across China","It never mentioned opium at all","It forced Britain to pay compensation to China","It returned Hong Kong Island to Chinese rule"], 1, "The treaty rewarded Britain with territory, ports and legal privileges, yet said nothing about opium, so the trade grew."],
    [3, "What did the treaties ending the Second Opium War do about opium?", ["Banned it throughout British-controlled territory","Placed the trade under direct Qing government control","Effectively legalised the trade","Limited sales to the single port of Canton"], 2, "After 1860 the opium trade was effectively legal, along with more ports and foreign envoys in Beijing."],
    [4, "Why do the Opium Wars still matter in China today?", ["They proved Western powers would respect Chinese sovereignty","They began a 'century of humiliation' still invoked today","They are rarely mentioned in Chinese schools or national messaging","They ended China's isolation on terms it welcomed"], 1, "Remembered as the start of a century of humiliation, the wars help explain China's focus on sovereignty."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A war fought over a banned drug still shapes how China sees the West."});

WB.L("thirty-years-war", "history", "advanced", "The Thirty Years' War",
  "It began with three men thrown from a castle window and became one of the deadliest wars in European history. Its peace treaties helped shape the idea of the modern state.",
  [
    ["Out the window in Prague", "A window-throwing in Prague in 1618 ignited a revolt that spread across the Holy Roman Empire's patchwork of states.",
      "In May 1618, angry Protestant nobles stormed Prague Castle, seized two Catholic officials of the Habsburg emperor and their secretary, and threw them from a high window. All three survived. Catholics said angels saved them; Protestants said they landed in a dung heap. The nobles were protesting Habsburg limits on Protestant rights in Bohemia. They rejected their Habsburg king and offered the crown to a Protestant, Frederick V of the Palatinate.\n\nRevolt spread easily because the Holy Roman Empire was a patchwork of hundreds of states, not a unified country, with an emperor of limited authority. Religious tension, rival dynasties and foreign powers eager to weaken the Habsburgs made it a tinderbox."],
    ["Catholic victories", "Habsburg forces crushed Bohemia in 1620 and then Denmark, and by the late 1620s the Catholic side seemed close to total victory.",
      "Frederick V's reign was so brief he became known as the Winter King. In 1620 imperial and Catholic League forces crushed his army at White Mountain, outside Prague. Bohemia was forcibly returned to Catholicism, Protestant nobles were executed or driven out, and their lands went to loyal supporters.\n\nKing Christian IV of Denmark, a Lutheran who also held lands within the empire, intervened in 1625 but was defeated. The emperor's armies were now partly led by Albrecht von Wallenstein, a brilliant and enormously wealthy military entrepreneur. He raised his own huge army and paid for it by forcing occupied regions to supply money and food."],
    ["Sweden and France", "Sweden's king turned the war around, and when Catholic France joined openly in 1635 it became a fight for power, not faith.",
      "In 1630 King Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden, a Lutheran, landed with a disciplined army, mobile field artillery and flexible infantry. Nicknamed the Lion of the North, he won at Breitenfeld in 1631, funded partly by Catholic France, which cared more about weakening the Habsburgs than about religion. He was killed at Lützen in 1632, though Sweden won the battle. Wallenstein, suspected of disloyalty, was assassinated by his own officers in 1634.\n\nIn 1635 France, guided by Cardinal Richelieu, openly entered the war, fighting alongside Protestant Sweden and German princes against the Catholic Habsburgs. France feared being encircled by Habsburg lands. The war was now mainly about the balance of power in Europe."],
    ["Hunger, plague and ruin", "Most deaths came from famine and disease, not battle, and German lands lost roughly a fifth or more of their people.",
      "Armies on all sides lived off the land, marching across the same German territories and plundering towns and villages again and again. The worst single atrocity was the sack of Magdeburg in 1631, when imperial troops stormed the Protestant city and it burned. About 20,000 people died, and the city was left almost empty.\n\nYet most deaths came from what armies brought with them: seized crops and livestock, burned farms, typhus and plague, and refugees crowded into towns where hunger followed. Estimates vary, but many historians think German lands lost roughly a fifth or more of their population, with some regions losing far more. Jacques Callot's etchings The Miseries of War and the novel Simplicius Simplicissimus captured the horror."],
    ["Westphalia and the state", "The 1648 peace redrew Europe and moved it towards competing sovereign states, though 'Westphalian sovereignty' is partly a later myth.",
      "Peace talks dragged on for years in two Westphalian towns, Münster and Osnabrück. The Peace of Westphalia in 1648 recognised the independence of the Dutch Republic and the Swiss Confederation, gave territory to France and Sweden, and allowed German princes greater freedom to run their own affairs. Calvinism was finally recognised alongside Lutheranism and Catholicism; the 1555 Peace of Augsburg had let each ruler choose only Lutheran or Catholic.\n\nPolitical scientists often trace 'Westphalian sovereignty' to here: the idea that states are equal and should not interfere in each other's internal affairs. Historians say this is partly a later myth. Even so, Europe shifted away from wars fought mainly over religion towards competing sovereign states."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why could a local revolt in Prague spread so widely?", ["The empire was a single centralised state with a strong standing army","The empire was a patchwork of hundreds of states, with religious tension and foreign rivals","Its rulers shared one faith and avoided any conflict","The Habsburg emperor had absolute authority"], 1, "A fragmented empire with religious and dynastic rivalries was a tinderbox, so one spark could set it alight."],
    [1, "What happened to Bohemia after the Battle of White Mountain?", ["It gained independence under King Frederick V","It was divided up between Sweden and Denmark","Catholicism returned by force and rebel nobles lost land","It received religious freedom from the emperor"], 2, "The Catholic victory ended the Bohemian revolt and stripped Protestant nobles of power and property."],
    [2, "Why did Catholic France fight alongside Protestant powers?", ["It wanted to weaken the Habsburgs and avoid encirclement, not defend a faith","Its cardinal had converted the kingdom to Protestantism","Sweden had conquered France in 1630","Its king wanted to restore Calvinism in Bohemia"], 0, "France's entry showed the war had become about power and the balance of Europe more than religion."],
    [3, "What caused most deaths in the Thirty Years' War?", ["Large pitched battles between rival armies","Naval blockades of the German coastline","Executions following the sack of Magdeburg","Hunger and disease, spread by plundering armies"], 3, "Looting, famine, typhus and plague killed far more people than the fighting itself."],
    [4, "How do historians treat the idea that Westphalia created modern state sovereignty?", ["As entirely accurate, since the treaties set out the modern state system","As entirely false, since the treaties changed nothing about Europe's borders","As settled fact, since the treaties created a single European government","As partly a later myth, though it marked a shift towards competing states"], 3, "The treaties were more complicated than the myth, but they did move Europe from religious wars to a states system."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Three men fell from a castle window and lived. Thirty years of war followed."});

WB.L("kokoda-track", "history", "beginner", "Kokoda: the track that shaped a nation",
  "In 1942, on a muddy jungle path in Papua, young Australian soldiers fought to stop a Japanese advance on Port Moresby. Kokoda became one of Australia's defining wartime stories.",
  [
    ["The threat from the north", "In 1942 Japan's advance brought war to Australia's doorstep, and Japanese troops landed in Papua to take Port Moresby overland.",
      "Early in 1942, Japan seemed unstoppable. Singapore fell in February, and thousands of Australian soldiers became prisoners of war. Days later Darwin was bombed. Papua was then an Australian territory, and its capital, Port Moresby, lay just across the sea from Cape York in Queensland.\n\nJapan wanted it as a base to threaten northern Australia and cut its links with the United States. An attempt by sea was turned back after the Battle of the Coral Sea in May 1942. So in July Japanese forces landed near Buna and Gona on Papua's northern coast, planning to cross the rugged Owen Stanley Range and seize Port Moresby from behind."],
    ["A track and its carriers", "The 96-kilometre Kokoda Track was a steep jungle footpath, and Papuan carriers kept the campaign supplied and carried out the wounded.",
      "The Kokoda Track runs about 96 kilometres between the outskirts of Port Moresby and the village of Kokoda. It is a footpath, used for generations by local people, that climbs and plunges over steep ridges and through dense rainforest. Soldiers crawled up slippery slopes, crossed fast rivers and slept in mud. Malaria, dysentery and exhaustion struck everyone.\n\nEvery bullet, tin of food and stretcher had to be carried in, mostly by thousands of Papuan men, some recruited under pressure. They hauled supplies forward and carried wounded Australians for days on stretchers, sheltering them at night. Sapper Bert Beros's poem called them the Fuzzy Wuzzy Angels, a name some now consider outdated. They were poorly paid, and many veterans felt their contribution was never properly honoured."],
    ["Militia and veterans", "Poorly trained young militia of the 39th Battalion held the first advance, then AIF veterans fought a costly withdrawal.",
      "The first Australians in the Japanese path were a small force of Papuan police and soldiers, and the 39th Battalion, a militia unit of young, mostly inexperienced volunteers and conscripts, many still in their teens. Some regulars had mocked militia as 'chocos', chocolate soldiers who would melt under pressure. On Kokoda they did nothing of the sort. Heavily outnumbered, they fought desperate actions around Kokoda village, then held at Isurava under Lieutenant Colonel Ralph Honner.\n\nVeterans of the Second AIF, who had fought in the Middle East, then joined the fight. Private Bruce Kingsbury charged forward firing a Bren gun from the hip and won a posthumous Victoria Cross, the first for action on Australian territory. The Australians could not hold Isurava, but made the Japanese pay for every ridge."],
    ["Turning the tide", "By late 1942 starving Japanese forces were ordered back, and Australians pushed them to the coast.",
      "By mid September the Japanese had reached Ioribaiwa Ridge, within sight of Port Moresby's lights according to many accounts. But they were starving and stretched thin at the end of a long supply line. They had also been defeated by Australians at Milne Bay and were locked in heavy fighting with the Americans at Guadalcanal. They were ordered to withdraw.\n\nAustralian troops pursued them back along the track, fighting hard battles at Eora Creek and Oivi to Gorari, and recaptured Kokoda on 2 November 1942. The campaign ended with bloody battles alongside American troops at Buna, Gona and Sanananda, which lasted into January 1943."],
    ["Kokoda in memory", "Kokoda is remembered as a fight to defend Australia itself, and today thousands walk the track each year.",
      "More than 600 Australians were killed on the Kokoda Track and over a thousand were wounded, with many more struck down by disease. Japanese losses were much greater, many from starvation and illness. Cameraman Damien Parer filmed the campaign, and his newsreel Kokoda Front Line won an Academy Award, bringing the jungle war home to cinema audiences.\n\nFor decades Kokoda received less attention than Gallipoli, but that began to change. In 1992 Prime Minister Paul Keating knelt and kissed the ground at Kokoda, arguing that Australians had fought there to defend their own country. Today thousands of Australians walk the track each year with Papuan guides, a hard trek that ends at memorials honouring both the soldiers and the carriers."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Japan want to capture Port Moresby?", ["To take control of Papua's mineral wealth","As a base to threaten northern Australia","To shelter its damaged fleet near the coast","To end Australian rule over New Guinea"], 1, "Port Moresby would have given Japan a base close to northern Australia and a way to isolate it from the United States."],
    [1, "What made the Kokoda Track so punishing?", ["Steep ridges, thick jungle, disease and no roads","A sealed road with heavy traffic and supply convoys","Open, dry desert with very little drinking water","Flat coastal swamps offering no cover from the air"], 0, "The terrain was as much an enemy as the army, and everything had to be carried on someone's back."],
    [2, "Why was the stand of the 39th Battalion so remarkable?", ["It was made up of veteran soldiers from the Middle East","It heavily outnumbered the Japanese force","Young, poorly trained militia, mocked as 'chocos', held a stronger force","It was supported by roads and heavy artillery"], 2, "Inexperienced young militia proved far tougher than their reputation suggested."],
    [3, "Why did the Japanese advance stall near Port Moresby?", ["Their forces were starving and stretched thin, with setbacks elsewhere","Australian reinforcements arrived by sea and overwhelmed them","Snow closed the mountain passes for the season","Their commanders switched the attack to Darwin"], 0, "A long supply line, hunger and defeats at Milne Bay and Guadalcanal led to the order to withdraw."],
    [4, "How do Australians remember Kokoda today?", ["As a minor campaign overshadowed by Gallipoli","As a battle won mostly by American troops","As an overseas adventure unrelated to home defence","As a fight to defend Australia itself, honoured by treks and memorials"], 3, "Kokoda is now seen as a defining stand to protect Australia, marked by pilgrimage walks and memorials."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Why a muddy jungle path in Papua became central to how Australians see themselves."});

WB.L("chernobyl", "history", "beginner", "Chernobyl: the night a reactor exploded",
  "In April 1986 a late night safety test went catastrophically wrong in Soviet Ukraine. The disaster spread radiation across Europe and shook the Soviet Union to its core.",
  [
    ["Test, flaw, explosion", "A rushed safety test and a flawed reactor design combined to destroy reactor 4 on 26 April 1986.",
      "The Chernobyl plant stood in northern Soviet Ukraine, near the new town of Pripyat. In the early hours of 26 April 1986, a night shift that had not prepared for it ran a test at reactor 4. They wanted to know whether spinning turbines could keep cooling pumps running briefly if power failed. Operators reduced the reactor's power and disabled several safety systems. It became unstable, and they withdrew most of the control rods.\n\nThe Soviet RBMK design had dangerous features operators were not fully told about: if cooling water turned to steam, power could rise rather than fall. When power surged, an operator hit the emergency shutdown, but a flaw in the rods briefly boosted the reaction. Steam explosions blew apart the reactor, and burning graphite sent radioactive material high into the sky for days."],
    ["Firefighters and liquidators", "Firefighters, pilots, miners and thousands of liquidators fought the aftermath, often unaware of how dangerous the radiation was.",
      "Firefighters raced to the plant within minutes and fought fires on the roof and around the reactor building. Few understood how intense the radiation was, and some reportedly picked up chunks of graphite thrown from the core. Two plant workers died on the night. In the following weeks and months, around 28 more, mostly firefighters and staff, died from acute radiation sickness, despite treatment in a specialist Moscow hospital.\n\nHelicopters dropped thousands of tonnes of sand, clay, lead and other materials onto the burning reactor to smother it. Later, miners dug beneath it to install cooling, and huge numbers of workers known as liquidators carried out the cleanup."],
    ["Pripyat and the Swedish alarm", "Pripyat was evacuated after about 36 hours, and the Soviet Union admitted the accident only after Sweden detected the fallout.",
      "On the day after the explosion, life in Pripyat continued almost as normal. Children played outside and a wedding went ahead. About 36 hours after the accident, residents were told to pack for a short evacuation. Roughly 49,000 people boarded buses believing they would return within days. They never did. The zone was widened to about 30 kilometres, and more than 100,000 people were eventually moved.\n\nSoviet authorities said almost nothing. On 28 April, workers arriving at Sweden's Forsmark plant, more than 1,000 kilometres away, set off radiation alarms. Swedish experts found nothing wrong at their plant and traced the particles to winds from the Soviet Union. Under pressure, Soviet state media issued a short statement that evening acknowledging an accident. Mikhail Gorbachev later suggested Chernobyl exposed the failings of the Soviet system."],
    ["Counting the cost", "Fallout spread across Europe, and the clearest health effect was child thyroid cancer, while the full death toll remains debated.",
      "Radioactive fallout drifted across Belarus, Ukraine and Russia and on to much of Europe, with rain bringing it down unevenly. Farmers as far away as Wales and Scandinavia faced restrictions on sheep and reindeer for years.\n\nThe clearest health effect was a sharp rise in thyroid cancer among people who were children at the time, mainly because radioactive iodine got into milk from cows grazing on contaminated grass. Thousands of cases were diagnosed, though most patients survived with treatment. Estimates of eventual cancer deaths vary widely. A major United Nations-led review in 2005 estimated up to about 4,000 deaths among the most heavily exposed groups, while some studies give much higher figures for Europe. Many evacuees also suffered serious stress and upheaval, and the economic cost ran into many billions of dollars."],
    ["The zone today", "A giant steel shelter now covers the reactor, and wildlife has returned to the empty exclusion zone.",
      "Within months, workers built a concrete shelter, the sarcophagus, over the shattered reactor. It was rushed and began to deteriorate, so an international project slid an enormous arched steel structure, the New Safe Confinement, over it in 2016. The last working reactor shut down in 2000.\n\nPripyat is now a ghost town, its apartment blocks crumbling. With people gone, the exclusion zone has become an unexpected wildlife refuge for wolves, elk, deer and rare Przewalski's horses, though scientists still debate radiation's effects on animals. In 2022 Russian troops occupied the site for several weeks during the invasion of Ukraine."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What combined to cause the Chernobyl explosion?", ["An earthquake that cracked the cooling pipes and pumps","A rushed test with safety systems off, and a flawed reactor design","Sabotage of the control rods by a night-shift operator","A gas leak that ignited beneath the reactor building"], 1, "Operator decisions during the test met a reactor design whose flaws operators had not been fully told about."],
    [1, "Why were so many early responders badly harmed?", ["Their protective suits failed in the heat of the fires","They ignored orders to stay away from the plant","Few understood how intense the radiation was","The radiation near the roof was lower than expected"], 2, "Responders did not grasp the radiation danger, and many later died of acute radiation sickness."],
    [2, "What forced the Soviet government to acknowledge the accident?", ["Radiation alarms at a Swedish nuclear plant","A public warning from residents of Pripyat","A request for help from the Ukrainian government","Photographs from foreign aircraft over the plant"], 0, "Sweden detected the fallout and traced it to the Soviet Union, ending the early silence."],
    [3, "What was Chernobyl's clearest health effect?", ["Mass radiation sickness across western Europe","A sharp rise in adult leukaemia across Wales","Widespread birth defects across northern Europe","A rise in child thyroid cancer, via contaminated milk"], 3, "Radioactive iodine in milk caused thousands of child thyroid cancers, though most patients survived."],
    [4, "Why was the original sarcophagus replaced?", ["It was damaged during the 2022 occupation","It was too small to cover the whole reactor","It was rushed and began to deteriorate","Wildlife in the zone had damaged it badly"], 2, "The hurried concrete shelter deteriorated, so the New Safe Confinement was built over it in 2016."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Sweden's alarms, not Moscow's, first told the world about Chernobyl."});

